{"version":18,"type":"full","exportedAt":1790484193613,"world":{"id":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","name":"The Salt Assize","description":"Muren Hollow, a tide-city where the water is a witness. Book one.","coverImageId":null,"theme":null,"continuityStaleThreshold":5,"calendar":null,"createdAt":1790346802978,"updatedAt":1790346802978},"mapLayers":[],"locationMarkers":[{"id":"9ayoLyQwuY6K3NvJJp5EV","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","mapLayerId":null,"linkedMapLayerId":null,"name":"Dogtooth Stair","description":"","x":0,"y":0,"imageId":null,"iconType":"landmark","tags":[],"factionId":null,"createdAt":1790347534458,"updatedAt":1790347534458,"version":1},{"id":"pwQ03bYK1kV2XvwIXJ8sO","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","mapLayerId":null,"linkedMapLayerId":null,"name":"The Lighterman's Rest","description":"","x":0,"y":0,"imageId":null,"iconType":"landmark","tags":[],"factionId":null,"createdAt":1790347537486,"updatedAt":1790347537486,"version":1},{"id":"trTWcw1PWewR49mB8Oe1k","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","mapLayerId":null,"linkedMapLayerId":null,"name":"The Salt Court","description":"","x":0,"y":0,"imageId":null,"iconType":"landmark","tags":[],"factionId":null,"createdAt":1790347531565,"updatedAt":1790347531565,"version":1}],"characters":[{"id":"DaUGAgn21c4UeY25ua0iK","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","name":"Mother Quill","aliases":[],"description":"The court's recorder. Writes down everything and remembers none of it, she says.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1790347328966,"updatedAt":1790347328966},{"id":"OShZgoWOMHOB-f2N1YASY","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","name":"Juno Skelling","aliases":[],"description":"The Salt Court's tide-reader. Keeps the tables, and has kept them honestly for nineteen years.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1790347324671,"updatedAt":1790347324671},{"id":"TtZBZzfxfUDD6Flj5B1bD","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","name":"Teodora Vance","aliases":[],"description":"","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1790346984035,"updatedAt":1790346984035},{"id":"mT4SGpNlFimxYBjPcjA3o","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","name":"Kesh Ardent-Bray","aliases":[],"description":"A lighterman of the Hollow, forty years on the water. Accused of holding a tax collector under until he stopped.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1790347322598,"updatedAt":1790347322598},{"id":"o8WmKmBuf_UosqIaZ519M","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","name":"Amaline Dusk","aliases":[],"description":"","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1790347178917,"updatedAt":1790347178917},{"id":"qs45DYV1I4mZBTBf7SSZj","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","name":"Hollis Rem","aliases":[],"description":"Collector of the salt duty. Drowned in the channel below Dogtooth Stair on the ninth of Greenrot.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1790347326810,"updatedAt":1790347326810}],"items":[],"characterSnapshots":[{"worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","characterId":"o8WmKmBuf_UosqIaZ519M","eventId":"Oxm1PcLpE3_ynSNULm86c","isAlive":true,"currentLocationMarkerId":null,"currentMapLayerId":null,"inventoryItemIds":[],"inventoryNotes":"","statusNotes":"","travelModeId":null,"id":"AfjQCETAyjyZhJs5lle5o","sortKey":1.000003,"createdAt":1790484004492,"updatedAt":1790484004492,"version":1},{"worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","characterId":"TtZBZzfxfUDD6Flj5B1bD","eventId":"UAjuj5iEMlLzRFkyPW9e1","isAlive":true,"currentLocationMarkerId":null,"currentMapLayerId":null,"inventoryItemIds":[],"inventoryNotes":"","statusNotes":"","travelModeId":null,"id":"jAu3QHYfK_WZWNsqAuhSD","sortKey":1,"createdAt":1790347004952,"updatedAt":1790347004952,"version":1}],"characterMovements":[],"itemPlacements":[],"locationSnapshots":[],"itemSnapshots":[],"relationships":[],"relationshipSnapshots":[],"timelines":[{"id":"Y352qYDJIu8d3SLh-JpxK","dayOffset":0,"worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","name":"The Assize of Low Water","description":"","color":"#6366f1","createdAt":1790346890703,"version":1}],"chapters":[{"id":"i8WcwkJsDTTd89n8PguPk","notes":"Standing when she should be sitting.","wordGoal":null,"worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","timelineId":"Y352qYDJIu8d3SLh-JpxK","number":1,"title":"Chapter 1","synopsis":"","createdAt":1790346890721,"updatedAt":1790484004545,"version":2}],"events":[{"id":"IfJT1MCLCTZqFq9vdCfd7","travelDays":null,"inWorldTime":null,"tension":null,"structureBeat":null,"status":"draft","povCharacterId":null,"isFlashback":false,"mentionedCharacterIds":[],"threadIds":[],"motifIds":[],"worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","chapterId":"i8WcwkJsDTTd89n8PguPk","timelineId":"Y352qYDJIu8d3SLh-JpxK","title":"A Floor That Slopes to the Water","description":"","locationMarkerId":"pwQ03bYK1kV2XvwIXJ8sO","involvedCharacterIds":["TtZBZzfxfUDD6Flj5B1bD","mT4SGpNlFimxYBjPcjA3o"],"involvedItemIds":[],"tags":[],"sortOrder":2,"createdAt":1790347967912,"updatedAt":1790348060909,"version":4},{"id":"Oxm1PcLpE3_ynSNULm86c","travelDays":null,"inWorldTime":null,"tension":null,"structureBeat":null,"status":"draft","povCharacterId":null,"isFlashback":false,"mentionedCharacterIds":[],"threadIds":[],"motifIds":[],"worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","chapterId":"i8WcwkJsDTTd89n8PguPk","timelineId":"Y352qYDJIu8d3SLh-JpxK","title":"Low Water at Four Eleven","description":"","locationMarkerId":"trTWcw1PWewR49mB8Oe1k","involvedCharacterIds":["TtZBZzfxfUDD6Flj5B1bD","o8WmKmBuf_UosqIaZ519M","mT4SGpNlFimxYBjPcjA3o","OShZgoWOMHOB-f2N1YASY","DaUGAgn21c4UeY25ua0iK"],"involvedItemIds":[],"tags":[],"sortOrder":3,"createdAt":1790347970811,"updatedAt":1790348020180,"version":2},{"id":"UAjuj5iEMlLzRFkyPW9e1","travelDays":null,"inWorldTime":null,"tension":null,"structureBeat":null,"status":"draft","povCharacterId":null,"isFlashback":false,"mentionedCharacterIds":["o8WmKmBuf_UosqIaZ519M","IZA9SgM62eniDEz1Z0O4i","qOWNCHIEzgIJWtQ8g8LPI"],"threadIds":[],"motifIds":[],"worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","timelineId":"Y352qYDJIu8d3SLh-JpxK","chapterId":"i8WcwkJsDTTd89n8PguPk","title":"The Court Takes the Tide","description":"","locationMarkerId":"trTWcw1PWewR49mB8Oe1k","involvedCharacterIds":["TtZBZzfxfUDD6Flj5B1bD","o8WmKmBuf_UosqIaZ519M","mT4SGpNlFimxYBjPcjA3o","DaUGAgn21c4UeY25ua0iK"],"involvedItemIds":[],"tags":[],"sortOrder":0,"createdAt":1790346890743,"updatedAt":1790348555417,"version":12},{"id":"_D85oPCtwoi_Sf8QLjgiL","travelDays":null,"inWorldTime":null,"tension":null,"structureBeat":null,"status":"draft","povCharacterId":null,"isFlashback":false,"mentionedCharacterIds":[],"threadIds":[],"motifIds":[],"worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","chapterId":"i8WcwkJsDTTd89n8PguPk","timelineId":"Y352qYDJIu8d3SLh-JpxK","title":"What the Stair Kept","description":"","locationMarkerId":"9ayoLyQwuY6K3NvJJp5EV","involvedCharacterIds":["TtZBZzfxfUDD6Flj5B1bD","OShZgoWOMHOB-f2N1YASY"],"involvedItemIds":[],"tags":[],"sortOrder":1,"createdAt":1790347683423,"updatedAt":1790348646408,"version":15}],"travelModes":[],"timelineRelationships":[],"crossTimelineArtifacts":[],"mapRoutes":[],"mapRegions":[],"mapRegionSnapshots":[],"mapAnnotations":[],"loreCategories":[],"lorePages":[],"factions":[],"factionMemberships":[],"factionRelationships":[],"knowledgeFacts":[],"knowledgeReveals":[],"characterGoals":[],"sceneTexts":[{"id":"7UPv420VmUmG3Ik7DURfs","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","eventId":"UAjuj5iEMlLzRFkyPW9e1","text":"The Salt Court stood on piles, and at low water you could hear it think. Teodora Vance had learned to time an argument by that sound — the long suck of the ebb under the floor, then a pause, then the boards easing as the weight came off them. An advocate who spoke into the pause was not heard. An advocate who waited was.\n\nShe waited now, both hands flat on the rail of the second bench, while the proctor read the charge into the damp.\n\n\"That on the ninth of Greenrot, at or about the hour of low water, the lighterman did hold under, until dead, the person of the collector Hollis Rem, in the channel below Dogtooth Stair.\"\n\nAmaline Dusk read it the way she read everything, as though the words were a length of rope she was paying out and would want back. She did not look at the accused once.\n\nTeodora looked. Kesh Ardent-Bray was a wide, weathered man of about sixty, and he had come to court in the coat he worked in, which was either honesty or the beginning of a defence. Salt had dried white along the seams. His hands were folded and enormous and perfectly still.\n\n\"How does he answer?\"\n\n\"He does not,\" said Teodora, and stood.\n\nThe room did what rooms do when a junior stands. Somewhere behind her a pen stopped, waited, and started again — the recorder in the corner, who had written down every word spoken in this building for thirty years and claimed to remember none of them.\n\n\"He does not answer,\" Teodora said, \"because the charge names an hour, and the hour is wrong.\"\n\n\"The charge names low water,\" said Dusk, without turning.\n\n\"It does. And the tide-table for the ninth of Greenrot gives low water at eleven minutes past four in the afternoon. Hollis Rem was seen alive on the stair at half past four by two witnesses the proctor has herself examined.\" She let the boards take the ebb. \"At half past four the channel below that stair was nine inches deep. A man cannot be held under nine inches of water by another man standing in it. He can only be held down, and holding down leaves marks on the shoulders, and the surgeon's account gives none.\"\n\nDusk turned round at last.\n\n\"You are arguing the water,\" she said.\n\n\"I am arguing the water,\" Teodora agreed. \"It is the only witness in Muren Hollow that has never been paid.\"","wordCount":416,"createdAt":1790347411785,"updatedAt":1790348486858},{"id":"U1Q-1wlOz4XH7Rmp8G33z","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","eventId":"Oxm1PcLpE3_ynSNULm86c","text":"Low water on the seventeenth came at eleven minutes past five, and the court sat through it. The boards eased. The pen behind Teodora waited and started again.\n\n\"The defence calls the tide-reader of this court.\"\n\nJuno Skelling took the stand as though it were the seventh step.\n\n\"Mistress Skelling. On the fourteenth of Greenrot, your tide-book left your keeping.\"\n\n\"It did.\"\n\n\"To whom?\"\n\n\"The proctor's clerk, signed for.\"\n\n\"And when it came back, was it as you gave it?\"\n\nSkelling looked at Amaline Dusk for the first time in nineteen years of not looking at anybody.\n\n\"No,\" she said. \"The ninth had been gone over. Four eleven had a tail on it. It read four fifty-one.\"\n\nThe room did not make a noise. That was the noise it made.\n\n\"Forty minutes,\" said Teodora. \"At four fifty-one the channel under Dogtooth Stair is four feet deep, and a man may be held under it by another man standing in it, and the charge is possible. At four eleven it is nine inches, and the charge is not possible, and my client is a lighterman who was taking on ballast in front of a yardman who has since been posted upriver.\"\n\n\"Advocate,\" said the bench.\n\n\"I have not finished, my lord, and I am nearly done. There is no forgery here. Nobody wrote a false number. Somebody put a tail on a one and made it a five, and then a man was charged with the hour instead of the deed, because the hour was the only thing that would hold him.\"\n\nAmaline Dusk stood.\n\n\"That is an accusation,\" she said.\n\n\"It is a tide-table,\" said Teodora. \"I am only reading it out.\"\n\nBehind her the pen stopped, and did not start again, and that was how she knew the room had believed her.","wordCount":303,"createdAt":1790348019369,"updatedAt":1790348019369},{"id":"poCFo9Rc5_32MxH_9dtMF","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","eventId":"IfJT1MCLCTZqFq9vdCfd7","text":"The Lighterman's Rest had a floor that sloped towards the water, so that anything spilled went home. Teodora sat on the low side with her back to the room and let Kesh Ardent-Bray decide how long to stand.\n\nHe stood a while.\n\n\"You'll want to know if I did it,\" he said at last.\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThat got him sat down.\n\n\"I want to know where you were at four eleven,\" Teodora said, \"and I want to know it in a way I can prove without you. If I prove it with you, the proctor will say you are a lighterman and lightermen lie about water the way priests lie about weather. So: no. I do not want to know if you did it.\"\n\nKesh put both hands on the table. In the light off the channel they looked like something that had been hauled up.\n\n\"I was at the Ordinary,\" he said. \"Taking on ballast. There is a book.\"\n\n\"There is always a book,\" said Teodora. \"Who signs it?\"\n\n\"The yardman.\"\n\n\"And where is the yardman?\"\n\nKesh looked at the table.\n\n\"He took a posting,\" he said. \"Upriver. Fourteenth of Greenrot.\"\n\nTeodora did not write that down, which took some doing.\n\n\"The fourteenth,\" she said.\n\n\"Aye.\"\n\n\"And Juno Skelling's tide-book went out to the proctor's clerk on the fourteenth, and came back two hours later.\"\n\n\"I wouldn't know about that.\"\n\n\"No,\" Teodora agreed. \"You wouldn't.\" She looked at the sloped floor, and at the dark line on the wall where the water had been often enough to leave a record of itself. \"Kesh. When they pulled Rem out from under this building, who was standing here?\"\n\n\"Half the Hollow.\"\n\n\"Who was standing here first?\"\n\nHe thought about it properly, which she liked him for.\n\n\"The proctor,\" he said. \"Before the hooks came. She had her boots off.\"\n\nTeodora sat very still, the way she did in court during the pause.\n\n\"She had her boots off,\" she repeated.\n\n\"Aye. Stood in it to her knees. I thought: there is a woman who does not mind the cold.\"","wordCount":346,"createdAt":1790347990933,"updatedAt":1790348060288},{"id":"uNhBI6OwEKxoXYfPg8LYq","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","eventId":"_D85oPCtwoi_Sf8QLjgiL","text":"The stair went down in eleven steps and the channel took the last four of them twice a day. Juno Skelling waited at the seventh, where the weed stopped, because below the seventh you could not stand and talk; you could only stand.\n\n\"You'll want the ninth,\" she said, before Teodora had asked.\n\n\"I want the ninth.\"\n\n\"Four eleven in the afternoon.\" Skelling did not consult anything. Nineteen years of tables were behind her eyes and she resented being asked to open them. \"Range of nine feet two. It was a spring tide, so the ebb ran hard until half past five and then it lay down.\"\n\n\"And the depth here, at half past four?\"\n\n\"Here.\" Skelling put her boot on the eighth step. \"A hand and a half. Call it nine inches.\" She looked up. \"You know all this. You have read my book.\"\n\n\"I have read your book,\" Teodora said. \"I want to know whether anybody else has.\"\n\nThe tide-reader's face did something small.\n\n\"The proctor's clerk had it out on the fourteenth,\" she said. \"Signed for it. Had it two hours and gave it back.\"\n\n\"Two hours is a long time with a book of numbers.\"\n\n\"It is a long time,\" Skelling agreed, \"if you are copying. It is no time at all if you are reading.\"\n\nBelow them the channel moved the way it moves when nothing is in it, which is to say it moved as if something were. Teodora looked at the water and thought about a man who had gone into it at a depth that would not drown a dog.\n\n\"Juno. If a body were put in here at half past four, where would it be at six?\"\n\n\"Under the Rest,\" said Skelling, without a pause. \"Every time. That is not a guess, that is the channel.\"\n\n\"And where was Hollis Rem found?\"\n\n\"Under the Rest,\" said Skelling. \"At six.\"\n\n\"Then he went in here,\" Teodora said, \"at half past four, and the charge is right about the place and wrong about everything else.\"\n\n\"That is not what I said.\"\n\n\"No,\" Teodora agreed. \"It is what the water said. I am only writing it down.\"","wordCount":363,"createdAt":1790347718240,"updatedAt":1790348646001}],"plotThreads":[],"motifs":[],"continuitySuppressions":[],"writingLogs":[{"id":"ZJV8oG93CnoF1PYLEd6Rn","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","date":"2026-09-25","words":1438,"createdAt":1790347411785,"updatedAt":1790348646001},{"id":"caisv2m4co4sceWu6YpBl","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","date":"2026-09-27","words":22,"createdAt":1790483836349,"updatedAt":1790484043109}],"sceneRevisions":[{"id":"166dBx1nv853yKv5k6aLW","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","eventId":"_D85oPCtwoi_Sf8QLjgiL","text":"The stair went down in eleven steps and the channel took the last four of them twice a day. Juno Skelling waited at the seventh, where the weed stopped, because below the seventh you could not stand and talk; you could only stand.\n\n\"You'll want the ninth,\" she said, before Teodora had asked.\n\n\"I want the ninth.\"\n\n\"Four eleven in the afternoon.\" Skelling did not consult anything. Nineteen years of tables were behind her eyes and she resented being asked to open them. \"Range of nine feet two. It was a spring tide, so the ebb ran hard until half past five and then it lay down.\"\n\n\"And the depth here, at half past four?\"\n\n\"Here.\" Skelling put her boot on the eighth step. \"A hand and a half. Call it nine inches.\" She looked up. \"You know all this. You have read my book.\"\n\n\"I have read your book,\" Teodora said. \"I want to know whether anybody else has.\"\n\nThe tide-reader's face did something small.\n\n\"The proctor's clerk had it out on the fourteenth,\" she said. \"Signed for it. Had it two hours and gave it back.\"\n\n\"Two hours is a long time with a book of numbers.\"\n\n\"It is a long time,\" Skelling agreed, \"if you are copying. It is no time at all if you are reading.\"\n\nBelow them the channel moved the way it moves when nothing is in it, which is to say it moved as if something were. Teodora looked at the water and thought about a man who had gone into it at a depth that would not drown a dog.\n\n\"Juno. If a body were put in here at half past four, where would it be at six?\"\n\n\"Under the Rest,\" said Skelling, without a pause. \"Every time. That is not a guess, that is the channel.\"\n\n\"And where was Hollis Rem found?\"\n\n\"Under the Rest,\" said Skelling. \"At six.\"\n\n\"Then he went in here,\" Teodora said, \"at half past four, and the charge is right about the place and wrong about everything else.\"\n\n\"That is not what I said.\"\n\n\"No,\" Teodora agreed. \"It is what the water said. I am only writing it down.\"","wordCount":363,"createdAt":1790348644146},{"id":"1amlk1olWRGxCTbbR50p5","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","eventId":"UAjuj5iEMlLzRFkyPW9e1","text":"@The Salt Court","wordCount":3,"createdAt":1790347412887},{"id":"2wFBQrZkMcjyaoWhYGZZ8","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","eventId":"IfJT1MCLCTZqFq9vdCfd7","text":"The Lighterman's Rest had a floor that sloped towards the water, so that anything spilled went home. Teodora sat on the low side with her back to the room and let Kesh Ardent-Bray decide how long to stand.\n\nHe stood a while.\n\n\"You'll want to know if I did it,\" he said at last.\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThat got him sat down.\n\n\"I want to know where you were at four eleven,\" Teodora said, \"and I want to know it in a way I can prove without you. If I prove it with you, the proctor will say you are a lighterman and lightermen lie about water the way priests lie about weather. So: no. I do not want to know if you did it.\"\n\nKesh put both hands on the table. In the light off the channel they looked like something that had been hauled up.\n\n\"I was at the Ordinary,\" he said. \"Taking on ballast. There is a book.\"\n\n\"There is always a book,\" said Teodora. \"Who signs it?\"\n\n\"The yardman.\"\n\n\"And where is the yardman?\"\n\nKesh looked at the table.\n\n\"He took a posting,\" he said. \"Upriver. Fourteenth of Greenrot.\"\n\nTeodora did not write that down, which took some doing.\n\n\"The fourteenth,\" she said.\n\n\"Aye.\"\n\n\"And Juno Skelling's tide-book went out to the proctor's clerk on the fourteenth, and came back two hours later.\"\n\n\"I wouldn't know about that.\"\n\n\"No,\" Teodora agreed. \"You wouldn't.\" She looked at the sloped floor, and at the dark line on the wall where the water had been often enough to leave a record of itself. \"Kesh. When they pulled Rem out from under this building, who was standing here?\"\n\n\"Half the Hollow.\"\n\n\"Who was standing here first?\"\n\nHe thought about it properly, which she liked him for.\n\n\"The proctor,\" he said. \"Before the hooks came. She had her boots off.\"\n\nTeodora sat very still, the way she did in court during the pause.\n\n\"She had her boots off,\" she repeated.\n\n\"Aye. Stood in it to her knees. I thought: there is a woman who does not mind the cold.\"","wordCount":346,"createdAt":1790347996160},{"id":"6c2Zs1f-ahHq60x5KJi49","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","eventId":"_D85oPCtwoi_Sf8QLjgiL","text":"[#Dogtooth Stair @@Teodora Vance @@Juno Skelling]\n\nThe stair went down in eleven steps and the channel took the last four of them twice a day. Juno Skelling waited at the seventh, where the weed stopped, because below the seventh you could not stand and talk; you could only stand.\n\n\"You'll want the ninth,\" she said, before Teodora had asked.\n\n\"I want the ninth.\"\n\n\"Four eleven in the afternoon.\" Skelling did not consult anything. Nineteen years of tables were behind her eyes and she resented being asked to open them. \"Range of nine feet two. It was a spring tide, so the ebb ran hard until half past five and then it lay down.\"\n\n\"And the depth here, at half past four?\"\n\n\"Here.\" Skelling put her boot on the eighth step. \"A hand and a half. Call it nine inches.\" She looked up. \"You know all this. You have read my book.\"\n\n\"I have read your book,\" Teodora said. \"I want to know whether anybody else has.\"\n\nThe tide-reader's face did something small.\n\n\"The proctor's clerk had it out on the fourteenth,\" she said. \"Signed for it. Had it two hours and gave it back.\"\n\n\"Two hours is a long time with a book of numbers.\"\n\n\"It is a long time,\" Skelling agreed, \"if you are copying. It is no time at all if you are reading.\"\n\nBelow them the channel moved the way it moves when nothing is in it, which is to say it moved as if something were. Teodora looked at the water and thought about a man who had gone into it at a depth that would not drown a dog.\n\n\"Juno. If a body were put in here at half past four, where would it be at six?\"\n\n\"Under the Rest,\" said Skelling, without a pause. \"Every time. That is not a guess, that is the channel.\"\n\n\"And where was Hollis Rem found?\"\n\n\"Under the Rest,\" said Skelling. \"At six.\"\n\n\"Then he went in here,\" Teodora said, \"at half past four, and the charge is right about the place and wrong about everything else.\"\n\n\"That is not what I said.\"\n\n\"No,\" Teodora agreed. \"It is what the water said. I am only writing it down.\"","wordCount":369,"createdAt":1790348379323},{"id":"Lxu0HqBjon9tw-7Klaz3m","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","eventId":"UAjuj5iEMlLzRFkyPW9e1","text":"The Salt Court stood on piles, and at low water you could hear it think. Teodora Vance had learned to time an argument by that sound — the long suck of the ebb under the floor, then a pause, then the boards easing as the weight came off them. An advocate who spoke into the pause was not heard. An advocate who waited was.\n\nShe waited now, both hands flat on the rail of the second bench, while the proctor read the charge into the damp.\n\n\"That on the ninth of Greenrot, at or about the hour of low water, the lighterman did hold under, until dead, the person of the collector Hollis Rem, in the channel below Dogtooth Stair.\"\n\nAmaline Dusk read it the way she read everything, as though the words were a length of rope she was paying out and would want back. She did not look at the accused once.\n\nTeodora looked. Kesh Ardent-Bray was a wide, weathered man of about sixty, and he had come to court in the coat he worked in, which was either honesty or the beginning of a defence. Salt had dried white along the seams. His hands were folded and enormous and perfectly still.\n\n\"How does he answer?\"\n\n\"He does not,\" said Teodora, and stood.\n\nThe room did what rooms do when a junior stands. Somewhere behind her a pen stopped, waited, and started again — the recorder in the corner, who had written down every word spoken in this building for thirty years and claimed to remember none of them.\n\n\"He does not answer,\" Teodora said, \"because the charge names an hour, and the hour is wrong.\"\n\n\"The charge names low water,\" said Dusk, without turning.\n\n\"It does. And the tide-table for the ninth of Greenrot gives low water at eleven minutes past four in the afternoon. Hollis Rem was seen alive on the stair at half past four by two witnesses the proctor has herself examined.\" She let the boards take the ebb. \"At half past four the channel below that stair was nine inches deep. A man cannot be held under nine inches of water by another man standing in it. He can only be held down, and holding down leaves marks on the shoulders, and the surgeon's account gives none.\"\n\nDusk turned round at last.\n\n\"You are arguing the water,\" she said.\n\n\"I am arguing the water,\" Teodora agreed. \"It is the only witness in Muren Hollow that has never been paid.\"","wordCount":416,"createdAt":1790348460674},{"id":"PKwjDvu0lh1pE_3zv0z1a","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","eventId":"UAjuj5iEMlLzRFkyPW9e1","text":"The Salt Court  stood on piles, and at low water you could hear it think. Teodora Vance had learned to time an argument by that sound — the long suck of the ebb under the floor, then a pause, then the boards easing as the weight came off them. An advocate who spoke into the pause was not heard. An advocate who waited was.\n\nShe waited now, both hands flat on the rail of the second bench, while the proctor read the charge into the damp.\n\n\"That on the ninth of Greenrot, at or about the hour of low water, the lighterman did hold under, until dead, the person of the collector Hollis Rem, in the channel below Dogtooth Stair.\"\n\nAmaline Dusk read it the way she read everything, as though the words were a length of rope she was paying out and would want back. She did not look at the accused once.\n\nTeodora looked. Kesh Ardent-Bray was a wide, weathered man of about sixty, and he had come to court in the coat he worked in, which was either honesty or the beginning of a defence. Salt had dried white along the seams. His hands were folded and enormous and perfectly still.\n\n\"How does he answer?\"\n\n\"He does not,\" said Teodora, and stood.\n\nThe room did what rooms do when a junior stands. Somewhere behind her a pen stopped, waited, and started again — the recorder in the corner, who had written down every word spoken in this building for thirty years and claimed to remember none of them.\n\n\"He does not answer,\" Teodora said, \"because the charge names an hour, and the hour is wrong.\"\n\n\"The charge names low water,\" said Dusk, without turning.\n\n\"It does. And the tide-table for the ninth of Greenrot gives low water at eleven minutes past four in the afternoon. Hollis Rem was seen alive on the stair at half past four by two witnesses the proctor has herself examined.\" She let the boards take the ebb. \"At half past four the channel below that stair was nine inches deep. A man cannot be held under nine inches of water by another man standing in it. He can only be held down, and holding down leaves marks on the shoulders, and the surgeon's account gives none.\"\n\nDusk turned round at last.\n\n\"You are arguing the water,\" she said.\n\n\"I am arguing the water,\" Teodora agreed. \"It is the only witness in Muren Hollow that has never been paid.\"\n\nThe Salt Court ","wordCount":419,"createdAt":1790347535459},{"id":"UC6fuP3eA0estahg8Z3o0","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","eventId":"_D85oPCtwoi_Sf8QLjgiL","text":"\n\nThe stair went down in eleven steps and the channel took the last four of them twice a day. Juno Skelling waited at the seventh, where the weed stopped, because below the seventh you could not stand and talk; you could only stand.\n\n\"You'll want the ninth,\" she said, before Teodora had asked.\n\n\"I want the ninth.\"\n\n\"Four eleven in the afternoon.\" Skelling did not consult anything. Nineteen years of tables were behind her eyes and she resented being asked to open them. \"Range of nine feet two. It was a spring tide, so the ebb ran hard until half past five and then it lay down.\"\n\n\"And the depth here, at half past four?\"\n\n\"Here.\" Skelling put her boot on the eighth step. \"A hand and a half. Call it nine inches.\" She looked up. \"You know all this. You have read my book.\"\n\n\"I have read your book,\" Teodora said. \"I want to know whether anybody else has.\"\n\nThe tide-reader's face did something small.\n\n\"The proctor's clerk had it out on the fourteenth,\" she said. \"Signed for it. Had it two hours and gave it back.\"\n\n\"Two hours is a long time with a book of numbers.\"\n\n\"It is a long time,\" Skelling agreed, \"if you are copying. It is no time at all if you are reading.\"","wordCount":220,"createdAt":1790347766215},{"id":"dnxGmgp6LnBTU8C-gNF_j","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","eventId":"_D85oPCtwoi_Sf8QLjgiL","text":"The stair went down in eleven steps and the channel took the last four of them twice a day. Juno Skelling waited at the seventh, where the weed stopped, because below the seventh you could not stand and talk; you could only stand.\n\n\"You'll want the ninth,\" she said, before Teodora had asked.\n\n\"I want the ninth.\"\n\n\"Four eleven in the afternoon.\" Skelling did not consult anything. Nineteen years of tables were behind her eyes and she resented being asked to open them. \"Range of nine feet two. It was a spring tide, so the ebb ran hard until half past five and then it lay down.\"\n\n\"And the depth here, at half past four?\"\n\n\"Here.\" Skelling put her boot on the eighth step. \"A hand and a half. Call it nine inches.\" She looked up. \"You know all this. You have read my book.\"\n\n\"I have read your book,\" Teodora said. \"I want to know whether anybody else has.\"\n\nThe tide-reader's face did something small.\n\n\"The proctor's clerk had it out on the fourteenth,\" she said. \"Signed for it. Had it two hours and gave it back.\"\n\n\"Two hours is a long time with a book of numbers.\"\n\n\"It is a long time,\" Skelling agreed, \"if you are copying. It is no time at all if you are reading.\"\n\nBelow them the channel moved the way it moves when nothing is in it, which is to say it moved as if something were. Teodora looked at the water and thought about a man who had gone into it at a depth that would not drown a dog.\n\n\"Juno. If a body were put in here at half past four, where would it be at six?\"\n\n\"Under the Rest,\" said Skelling, without a pause. \"Every time. That is not a guess, that is the channel.\"\n\n\"And where was Hollis Rem found?\"\n\n\"Under the Rest,\" said Skelling. \"At six.\"\n\n\"Then he went in here,\" Teodora said, \"at half past four, and the charge is right about the place and wrong about everything else.\"\n\n\"That is not what I said.\"\n\n\"No,\" Teodora agreed. \"It is what the water said. I am only writing it down.\"","wordCount":363,"createdAt":1790347914699}],"tombstones":[{"id":"FhZItVJUkHHpiSnd6Sy6K","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","entityType":"character","entityId":"qOWNCHIEzgIJWtQ8g8LPI","version":1,"deviceId":"HFZ8wQZkBZcXa_6MTyyDu","deletedAt":1790347512554},{"id":"IC5s6CQViWY7i1Nty6a5R","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","entityType":"event","entityId":"-pk9jKcDOZCXCrCht0QH0","version":6,"deviceId":"HFZ8wQZkBZcXa_6MTyyDu","deletedAt":1790484129178},{"id":"kB-2G3GO9py-cpmY4Q0hi","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","entityType":"event","entityId":"feRL55eqb7HWFbknvRH-K","version":1,"deviceId":"HFZ8wQZkBZcXa_6MTyyDu","deletedAt":1790348327743},{"id":"tCPmkFsg1nBGR9sQcsxYo","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","entityType":"event","entityId":"93CKNRp33Vs-VOm1quIpr","version":3,"deviceId":"HFZ8wQZkBZcXa_6MTyyDu","deletedAt":1790483893575},{"id":"zdtL5XeENR97xrbMPDYr6","worldId":"Ia8ApQr_QoY41ozxI4euT","entityType":"character","entityId":"IZA9SgM62eniDEz1Z0O4i","version":1,"deviceId":"HFZ8wQZkBZcXa_6MTyyDu","deletedAt":1790347507402}],"blobs":[]}